Here is how to deploy your site live to the web, swap themes, and customize layouts and styles.

Step 1: Make It Public on GitHub Pages
Commit and push your current changes:
Open a terminal, make sure all your recent Gemfile edits and .gitignore rules are pushed:

DOS
git add Gemfile Gemfile.lock .gitignore _config.yml
git commit -m "Update Gemfile dependencies and ignore rules"
git push origin main
Configure GitHub Pages settings:

Go to your repository on GitHub.com.

Ensure the repository visibility is Public (Go to Settings → scroll to the Danger Zone at the bottom → change visibility to Public if it is currently Private).

In the left sidebar under Code and automation, click Pages.

Under Build and deployment:

Source: Select Deploy from a branch.

Branch: Choose main (or master) and directory / (root).

Click Save.

Check the live deployment:

Click the Actions tab at the top of your GitHub repository. You will see a workflow named pages build and deployment.

Once it shows a green checkmark (usually 30–60 seconds), your site will be live at:

Plaintext
https://<your-username>.github.io/<repo-name>/
(If your repository is named <your-username>.github.io, the URL will simply be https://<your-username>.github.io/).